THINK part gear-blue-U (35185 Technic Gear 20 Tooth Double Bevel with Clutch on Both Sides): on axle-input-T, meshing upward with gear-gray-L; free
THINK touches axle-input-T turns in bracket-G's round hole~
THINK touches gear-blue-U's round hole goes onto axle-input-T~
THINK touches gear-blue-U's teeth mesh with gear-gray-L's teeth
THINK does gear-blue-U drives gear-gray-L (ratio 20/16)
THINK need gear_pair: Passes turning from one parallel shaft to another. Changes speed and force by the tooth ratio and reverses direction. Rules: Gears must be locked to their shafts (cross hole on axle). Shafts must turn freely (round holes) and be held at the right distance: (teeth_a + teeth_b) / 16 holes for spur gears (e.g. 16+16 = 2 holes, 8+24 = 2, 12+20 = 2). Each shaft needs support on two points or the gears push apart.
THINK why A double-bevel gear meshes cleanly with gear-gray-L's plain teeth at this close spacing; its own clutch faces aren't used here, just its teeth.
THINK role axle-input-T in the gear shaft and clutch: Input shaft, turned by hand or motor at ext-V; meshes gear-blue-U into gear-gray-L.
THINK role gear-blue-U in the gear shaft and clutch: Input gear on axle-input-T; drives gear-gray-L.
THINK check axle-input-T.axle1 goes into bracket-G.hole1, 1 hole deep, turns freely (estimated from the picture)
THINK check axle-input-T.axle2 goes into gear-blue-U.hole1, 1 hole deep, turns freely (estimated from the picture)
THINK builds_on axle-input-T builds on bracket-G (MOC-183729-step-05), which is the input shaft bearing post in the gearbox housing, and rests on pin-blue-D (MOC-183729-step-04)
THINK builds_on gear-blue-U builds on gear-gray-L (MOC-183729-step-07), which is the driven gear in the input to main shaft, and rests on axle-N (MOC-183729-step-07)
THINK mechanism axle-input-T becomes the input shaft of the input to main shaft
THINK mechanism gear-blue-U becomes the driver gear of the input to main shaft
THINK state input_mesh has 4 of 4 parts, complete
THINK later axle-input-T's free end takes an output-style extension in step 11, making it an external handle. This is the gearbox's drive input. Pattern: input gear mesh
ADD axle-input-T 3705 black
ADD gear-blue-U 35185 blue
CONNECT axle-input-T.axle1 -> bracket-G.hole1~ TURNS
CONNECT axle-input-T.axle2 -> gear-blue-U.hole1~ TURNS
MESH gear-blue-U.teeth gear-gray-L.teeth
DOES gear-blue-U DRIVES gear-gray-L
RATIO gear-blue-U gear-gray-L 20/16
PATTERN input_gear_mesh
MECH input_mesh gear_pair axle-input-T input_shaft
MECH input_mesh gear_pair gear-blue-U driver_gear
STATE input_mesh 4/4 COMPLETE

STEP 11
THINK have Input shaft (axle-input-T, gear-blue-U) meshes with the main shaft's gear-gray-L; the dog-clutch sleeve sits between gear-gray-L and gear-red-P.
THINK goal Finishes both shaft ends as external handles a motor or another model can plug into.
THINK part ext-V (6538b Technic Axle Connector Ridged): on axle-input-T's free end, sticking out the bottom of the frame as a handle; fixed
THINK part ext-W (6538b Technic Axle Connector Ridged): on axle-N's free end, sticking out the top-right of the frame as the output; fixed
THINK touches ext-V's cross hole locks onto axle-input-T's free end
THINK touches ext-W's cross hole locks onto axle-N's free end~
THINK does axle-input-T carries ext-V
THINK does axle-N carries ext-W
THINK need shaft_with_stops: An axle that carries parts and turns, held from sliding by stops at both ends. Rules: At least two round-hole bearings. A stop on each side of the bearings.
THINK why A ridged axle connector gives a longer, grippable stub with a cross hole on its far end, so the shaft can reach past the frame and still take another axle.
THINK role ext-V in the gear shaft and clutch: Input handle, the exposed end of axle-input-T.
THINK role ext-W in the gear shaft and clutch: Output handle, the exposed end of axle-N.
THINK check axle-input-T.axle3 goes into ext-V.axlehole1, 1 hole deep, locked (turn together) (estimated from the picture)
THINK check axle-N.axle8 goes into ext-W.axlehole1, 1 hole deep, locked (turn together) (estimated from the picture)
THINK builds_on ext-V builds on axle-input-T (MOC-183729-step-10), which is the input shaft in the input to main shaft, and rests on bracket-G (MOC-183729-step-05), gear-blue-U (MOC-183729-step-10)
THINK builds_on ext-W builds on axle-N (MOC-183729-step-07), which is the main shaft, carries the clutch and both output gears in the input to main shaft, and rests on bush-K (MOC-183729-step-07), gear-gray-L (MOC-183729-step-07)
THINK mechanism ext-W becomes the output handle of the output shaft
THINK state output has 3 of 3 parts, complete
THINK result Turn axle-N once: gear-tan-M turns 1 turn, ext-W turns 1 turn
THINK later Nothing further in this 11-step booklet -- this is the last step. ext-V is where you'd turn the gearbox by hand or motor; ext-W is where the selected speed comes out. Pattern: axle extension handle
ADD ext-V 6538b white
ADD ext-W 6538b white
CONNECT axle-input-T.axle3 -> ext-V.axlehole1~ LOCKED
CONNECT axle-N.axle8 -> ext-W.axlehole1~ LOCKED
DOES axle-input-T CARRIES ext-V
DOES axle-N CARRIES ext-W
PATTERN axle_extension
MECH output shaft_with_stops ext-W output_handle
STATE output 3/3 COMPLETE
MOTION axle-N gear-tan-M=1 ext-W=1
